await (async (page) => { const expected={"number":"270","title":"Trent Horn vs Dan Barker — Does the Christian God Exist? (2018)","titleWithoutYear":"Trent Horn vs Dan Barker — Does the Christian God Exist?","eventYear":2018,"motion":"Does the Christian God exist?","cards":35,"tags":4,"firstRole":"Constructive","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bIuDfh-6iUs","speakers":["Trent Horn","Dan Barker"],"positions":["Christian theism","Atheism"],"cardDescriptions":["Because an infinite past could never be traversed, the universe began and requires an uncaused, timeless, immaterial, powerful personal cause possessing central divine attributes.","By defining power as physical work over time, Barker argues that an immaterial pure actuality cannot produce physical change, challenging Horn’s claim that a powerful divine actualizer exists.","Because every present change requires actualization, an instrumental regress must terminate in pure actuality, implying an immaterial, timeless, necessary, powerful, knowing, and good divine actualizer.","If functional complexity requires explanation, God’s ordered mind also needs one, so ending the explanatory chain with God risks special pleading rather than resolving dependence.","Because brute existence and endlessly dependent universes leave contingency unexplained, the universe requires a necessary being of pure actuality that explains why anything exists.","Because physical vacua yield particles without an external divine cause, the underdefined idea of something arising from nothing does not uniquely support Horn’s creation argument.","Quantum vacua are active fields, change needs actualization, mental agency illustrates causal power, and a necessary first cause differs explanatorily from the dependent things it explains.","Because locomotives require fuel and mental composition has physical realization, Horn’s analogies do not demonstrate that an immaterial God can exercise uncaused power without external support.","Because early testimony reports appearances and an empty tomb despite contrary expectations, Jesus’ bodily resurrection best explains the evidence and identifies the divine cause as Christian.","Because resurrection accounts conflict and receive no independent historical corroboration beyond believers, their testimony is too unreliable to provide strong evidence for the Christian God.","Because the Gospels share an empty-tomb and appearance core while resurrection belief arose early, minor variations and comparisons with later miracle legends do not eliminate the need for explanation.","Because Barker proposed private grief-related inner experiences rather than a shared hallucination, Horn’s group-hallucination rebuttal misses a plausible natural alternative and does not force a miracle.","Shared empty-tomb details and Paul's reports of appearances to named individuals and groups support resurrection, so narrative differences and a private grief experience remain incomplete alternatives.","Women were expected tomb visitors, while conflicting accounts identify different visitors, times, and tomb-opening scenes, so their presence adds little authentication and the discrepancies weaken credibility.","Appearance vocabulary, vanishing episodes, and Paul's later experience fit a visionary interpretation, so reports that Jesus appeared do not independently establish an ordinary bodily resurrection.","Figurative accommodation, ancient conflation of causation with permission, and surviving peoples after total-destruction claims support nonliteral readings, so troubling passages need not depict immoral divine action.","Biblical commands and depictions attribute profoundly harmful conduct to a supposedly good God, so the Bible's own good-fruit standard makes that divine character internally contradictory.","Because scripture contains distinct genres and later portrays Amalekites surviving supposed total destruction, conquest language need not describe literal extermination and cannot alone establish genocide.","Changing the label from evil to disaster leaves harmful conduct unjustified, while reported Israelite child sacrifice weakens any moral contrast used to condemn Canaanites.","Some biblical evil language denotes natural disaster rather than moral wrongdoing, and divine power can bring greater goods and eternal happiness from finite suffering.","If divinely ordered total group killing literally occurred and qualifies as genocide, defining God as perfect goodness must explain the act rather than merely relabel it.","Independent grounds for God and resurrection permit troubling passages to be human additions, nonliteral accounts, or exercises of divine authority over mortal life before eternity.","Horn classifies God's existence as philosophical rather than scientific because science addresses observable phenomena, so audiences must evaluate philosophical arguments instead of expecting scientific consensus.","Persistent debate and reliance on human advocates are unexpected if a revelation-capable God wants belief, so divine non-obviousness provides evidence against the Christian God's existence.","Evil remains logically compatible with God if suffering permits greater goods such as genuine freedom, so suffering alone does not deductively disprove divine existence.","Severe childhood suffering, apparently chance-like prayer outcomes, and flourishing unbelievers conflict with expected divine protection and weaken claims that belief in God is practically necessary.","God may permit nonbelief because constant awareness of divine surveillance could constrain free conduct, so religious disagreement does not constitute a strict logical contradiction with divine existence.","Claims that God answers prayer and physically affects events create observable differences from a godless universe, so Barker argues God's existence is scientifically testable.","Prayer that correctly reveals concealed writing would offer striking public evidence for divine knowledge, while refusal weakens claims that broad biblical prayer promises are testable.","Objective moral obligations transcend social opinion and cannot arise from merely descriptive natural laws, so universal moral commands require a perfect personal source identified as God.","Systemic clerical abuse and institutional protection of offenders count against a church claimed as divine revelation, because such corruption is unexpected under good divine guidance.","A corrupt doctor's wrongdoing does not invalidate medicine, so misconduct by church members likewise need not falsify the church's spiritual claims or justify abandoning it.","The doctor analogy fails because Barker alleges coordinated institutional protection rather than isolated misconduct, preserving church-wide corruption as evidence against claims of divine revelation.","Universal moral facts arise from natural law grounded in God's perfect nature rather than arbitrary orders or harm aggregation, whereas Horn says atheism permits only moral opinion.","Real-world harm can provide objective justification for secular moral judgments, and Job's suffering tests divine goodness even if God promises eventual unlimited compensation afterward."],"overallLinks":[{"label":"Red herring","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/"},{"label":"Confirmation bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"},{"label":"Equivocation","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"}],"debateId":"horn-barker-christian-god-2018"}; return (async(page)=>{
